"""
Blockchain Complex · EMERGENCY VIOLENCE DETECTION + COUNTERMEASURES  (v4, merged)
================================================================================

WHAT THIS DOES
    An IoT device asks for help with a possible real-time violent incident near
    a person. In one sub-second call this module: takes the waiver + payment
    (emergencies are FREE), pulls the data, wakes the other IoTs that would
    normally just watch, works out who is the victim and who is the aggressor,
    decides whether an attack is real and imminent, runs a parallel
    cyber-physical integrity scan, and - only when it is needed and would work -
    runs DEFENSIVE countermeasures under a hard Red Line that never endangers
    law enforcement or bystanders. If nothing is in range, it can act through
    the requesting IoT itself while it searches, sub-second, for a more distant
    IoT able to deploy the needed signal at LIGHT SPEED (frequency / RF).

COORDINATE CONTRACT
    Every position - Actor.where and Scene.safe_exits - is in a LOCAL frame
    whose ORIGIN is the acting IoT / the emitter of the directional measure, so
    a position vector IS its direction from the emitter and red_line_ok can
    compare directions with _angle(...). If a measure is emitted from a
    DIFFERENT device, pass that device's position as `emitter`; the angle is
    then taken at that vertex with _angle_from(emitter, ...). Zero-length or
    non-finite vectors are undefined and never fail a Red Line check.

SCOPE AND SAFETY  (read first)
    - Aggressor-facing measure: a loud ATTENTION alarm + high-visibility lights
      to deter, draw witnesses and summon help. This module NEVER computes or
      emits an incapacitating, disabling or injurious signal.
    - Victim-facing measure: a biometric-aware prompt ("RUN", a direction to the
      safest exit) that points the victim AWAY from the aggressor.
    - Red Line: a directional measure is withheld if aimed within 35 deg of a
      responder or bystander; an area alarm is suppressed if law enforcement is
      on scene, so it never interferes with their operation.
    - Fail-safe billing: emergencies are FREE; a failed or handed-off request is
      charged ZERO.
    Real deployment needs lawful authority, each IoT maker's agreement, real
    sensor fusion to tell victim from aggressor, and review by counsel.

PAYMENT  (company-wide)
    data a peer shares         -> data units,           $BTC
    neural networks a peer runs -> neural-network units, $BCH

PROVENANCE
    Draft (Claude) -> review (Google) -> tightening (Grok) -> merged.
    Grok's constants, input validation, None-safe angles, cyber-integrity
    confidence down-weighting, area-alarm fallback and logging; plus the draft's
    distant-RF light-speed search, the "requesting IoT acts itself" path, and
    the _angle_from emitter geometry (Grok's own suggestion).
"""
